  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday morning through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 147 PM EDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nWell-above normal temperatures will persist for one more day to \nstart the long term period. After beginning the morning with low \ntemperatures in the low to mid 60s once again, highs on Friday \nafternoon are forecast to rise into the low to mid 80s in north \nGeorgia and upper 80s in central Georgia. Over the course of the \nday, a strong low pressure system will move from the central Great \nLakes towards the far northeast CONUS, advancing a strong cold front \ntowards north Georgia. With little opportunity for deep moisture \nreturn ahead of this front, rain chances will be limited, with only \nsome isolated showers in far north Georgia on Friday night. The \nfront will quickly progress southward overnight, clearing the \nforecast area to the southeast by sunrise on Saturday. \n\nThe big story for us is that a significantly cooler airmass will \nfilter into the area in the wake of the front, giving us our first \nreal taste of Fall for the year. Low temperatures on Saturday will \nbe in the mid to upper 40s to the north of I-85 and in the 50s to \nthe south. As cooler air becomes more established, afternoon highs \nwill range from near 60 in the far northern tier to the low 70s in \nsouth-central Georgia. An upper level ridge will build over the \neastern CONUS and the center of a strong surface high will move \nacross the lower Midwest on Saturday night into Sunday. Strong \nradiational cooling under clear skies will lead to the coolest \ntemperatures of the period early Sunday morning, with widespread 40s \nand even some upper 30s in the higher elevations of far northeast \nGeorgia. As the surface high moves east of the Appalachians, a CAD \nwedge is forecast to keep highs limited to the low 60s in portions \nof northeast Georgia compared to mid to upper 60s elsewhere across \nthe region. Breezy easterly winds will also be present within this \nwedge. A gradual warming trend will begin to start next week as the \nsurface high moves into the west Atlantic and the aforementioned \nridge becomes centered over the Southeast and Mid-Atlantic coast.",
